WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.000
Ben: Hello, I'm Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.450
A constitutional amendment to balance the
budget is a key item in the Republican contract

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:13.469
with America.

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:18.789
The House of Representatives recently passed
it now the debate moves to the Senate.

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:23.810
But do we really need to change the U.S. Constitution
to get our books in order?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.570
How important is a balanced budget?

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.230
And is there a political agenda behind the
debate?

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.000
Joining us to sort through the conflict and
the consensus are four leading economists

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.110
from San Francisco via satellite Milton Friedman,
Nobel laureate in economics and senior research

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:44.150
fellow at the Hoover Institution.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:49.450
Herbert Stein, Chairman of the Council of
Economic Advisers under Presidents Nixon and

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.820
Ford, and a senior fellow at the American
Enterprise Institute.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.430
James Miller, Budget Director for President
Reagan and John M. Olin Distinguished Fellow

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.750
at the Center for the Study of Public Choice
at George Mason University.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.720
And Henry Aaron, Director of Economic Studies
at the Brookings Institution, and a former

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:12.890
chairman of the Advisory Council on Social
Security.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.550
The topic before this house, the budget-balancing
act.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:34.190
This week on "Think Tank."

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:38.751
One of the most common stories in the economic
profession concerned President Truman, who

00:01:38.751 --> 00:01:44.570
after hearing so many economists tell him,
on the one hand this, and on the other hand

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:50.620
that, turned to his staff and growled, "Find
me a one-armed economist," and they're probably

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.000
still out looking.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.799
In the 1980s, the federal deficit went way
up and stayed up.

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:03.611
According to the budget just introduced by
the Clinton administration, in 1995 the government

00:02:03.611 --> 00:02:11.000
will spend $193 billion more than it will
take in and the deficit will continue at about

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.549
that level into the future.

00:02:13.549 --> 00:02:21.280
Moreover, critics say Congress won't stop
this trend because Congress is out of control.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.930
House Republicans say the best way to rein
in spending is to pass a constitutional amendment

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:33.000
that will force Congress to balance the budget
by the year 2002.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:36.079
What they're saying is, "Stop me before I
kill again."

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:40.480
But is the deficit really so bad?

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.529
For the nation as a whole, the deficit as
a share of our gross domestic product is heading

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:47.319
down.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:50.300
So on the one hand, things don't look so bad.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.590
In fact, some Democrats say that the crusade
for a balanced budget amendment is really

00:02:54.590 --> 00:03:00.599
just a smokescreen for a conservative ideology
that wants to strangle government by cutting

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:04.269
off its oxygen, that is, its ability to spend.

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:10.129
But on the other hand, look at this, the Bipartisan
Commission on Entitlement and Tax Reform projects

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:17.389
that unless changes are made, the deficit
will soar to 19% of our gross domestic product

00:03:17.389 --> 00:03:22.629
by the year 2030, and that would be a handful.

00:03:22.629 --> 00:03:28.760
Milton Friedman in San Francisco via satellite,
do we need a balanced budget amendment?

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:35.199
Milton: We need a strong balanced budget amendment
such as was proposed in the Contract for America

00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:37.950
with a tough tax limitation provision.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:44.109
We do not need the kind of weak balanced budget
amendment that was actually passed by the

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:45.109
Congress.

00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:46.109
Ben: Okay, Herbert Stein.

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:51.730
Herbert: Well, I think you're gonna find that
you need some two-handed economists here because

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.340
I don't think we need a balanced budget amendment
either.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.159
I don't think we need a balanced budget amendment
of either kind.

00:03:57.159 --> 00:04:02.040
I think our record, except for a brief spree
during the Reagan period, was not so bad.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.390
I think we are learning to do better.

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.180
And I think that we will do better without
the amendment than with it.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:09.299
Ben: Henry Aaron.

00:04:09.299 --> 00:04:11.870
Henry: I think we need to balance the budget.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:16.530
I think we can balance the budget, in fact,
by the year 2002.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:21.660
But I think a balanced budget amendment will
do absolutely nothing to help us along the

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:29.699
way and will, in short order, be the source
of incentives for gimmickry.

00:04:29.699 --> 00:04:36.960
It will bring the courts into budget policy
in a way that I think everybody should deplore.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.389
And in short, I think the Balanced Budget
Amendment will stand in our way rather than

00:04:41.389 --> 00:04:44.349
help us on the road to sound budget policy.

00:04:44.349 --> 00:04:45.349
Ben: Jim Miller.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.650
James: I disagree with all three of my colleagues
here.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:54.960
I think we need a balanced budget amendment,
though I would support the one that Milton

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:55.960
was talking about.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.289
I don't think we ought to cast this one away.

00:04:57.289 --> 00:05:01.800
I think it would have some good effects some
research that I've done with Mark Crane suggests

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.930
that.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.999
On the other hand, I think it would have an
effect.

00:05:04.999 --> 00:05:07.110
It's not that it would just be gimmickry.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.930
I think the result would be that it would
put discipline on members of Congress, the

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:12.909
sort of discipline that they sorely need.

00:05:12.909 --> 00:05:13.909
Ben: But does...

00:05:13.909 --> 00:05:14.909
Go ahead.

00:05:14.909 --> 00:05:18.770
Herbert: Well, it kind of assumes that this
discipline will be in the direction of getting

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.720
us a better budget policy.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.480
Now, there is no reason to think that the
best of all budget policies is to balance

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.780
the budget every year or every decade or every
generation.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:33.360
We're not gonna have the best of all budget
policies anyway, with or without the amendment.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.659
The question is whether, without the amendment,
we will have a chance of coming closer?

00:05:38.659 --> 00:05:46.440
And I think we will because the economy fluctuates,
cyclically, demography changes, other requirements

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:47.440
change.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.939
And the Balanced Budget Amendment is gonna
prevent us from making rationalize adaptations

00:05:51.939 --> 00:05:53.650
to these changes in the economy.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.810
Milton: I don't believe that's correct.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:02.620
The balance...the amendment as it was originally
proposed did not rule out temporary deficits

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:03.949
or surpluses.

00:06:03.949 --> 00:06:07.550
It did not require a balanced budget every
year.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:12.240
The issue, however, is not being, it seems
to me, presented correctly.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.180
The key issue is not balancing the budget.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:20.939
The key issue is how do you keep spending
from growing at an intolerable rate?

00:06:20.939 --> 00:06:28.439
As of today, governments, federal, state and
local spend about 43% of the national income

00:06:28.439 --> 00:06:30.599
we're not getting our money's worth.

00:06:30.599 --> 00:06:35.110
And the trouble with a weak Balanced Budget
Amendment, which is why I am against it, is

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:36.759
that it will tend to drive Congress.

00:06:36.759 --> 00:06:41.620
It will be just, it will have discipline as
Jim says, but will tend to exert that discipline

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.659
in the way of higher taxes rather than lower
spending.

00:06:44.659 --> 00:06:49.030
Henry: If that's what you believe Milton,
then I think you should make your case to

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:55.490
the American public, and try and win it in
a straight up fight in a democratic system,

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:02.229
not shackle the budgetary process with rules
that are really not made for the next year

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:04.460
or the next five years but for decades.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.050
Ben: But Henry, let me ask you this.

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:11.830
We have rarely had an election, certainly
in a congressional year where the issues were

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.810
at least allegedly nationalized.

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:18.590
Now, Gingrich went around with that Contract
for America, and he said, "This is what we're

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:19.590
voting on."

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:20.590
Herbert: Oh, but...

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.319
Ben: Well, wait a minute that's what he said.

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:24.970
I know there's a lot of flaws in there but
he said...

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:28.280
Henry: But 30% of the public had never heard
of the contract and most of those who had

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.930
heard of it didn't know what it said.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:30.930
Milton: But this is...

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.990
Henry: So it's not a...that wasn't even an
example of a nationalization.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:41.479
Let me say the reason I think good conservatives
should be really upset at the prospect of

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:46.210
a balanced budget amendment, as many conservatives
are, such as Robert Bork, the editorial board

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:48.060
of the "Wall Street Journal."

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.830
Is that if we enact such an amendment, it
is going to threaten I think one of the great

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:57.999
strengths of the American economy, that conservatives
would correctly pride.

00:07:57.999 --> 00:08:03.840
Compared to European nations, we have less
government spending, we are less bureaucratic,

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.740
we have fewer regulations.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:11.669
It may be the case that we are in the midst
of a conservative era currently, in a few

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:17.139
years there may be a more activist mood in
Congress, what would be the easiest thing

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:18.800
in the world to do?

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:26.499
Well, mandate, that private business, do the
things that government now spends, pay unemployment

00:08:26.499 --> 00:08:33.000
insurance benefits, pay retirement benefits,
provide sickness benefits.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:39.030
One could, in other words, replace, because
of the restrictions of a balanced budget amendment,

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:45.630
the tendency when there is a consensus about
public need for government to spend money

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.000
and raise taxes, with regulations, and mandates.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.070
Ben: And here you're talking about what good
conservatives should believe, you certainly

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:53.410
have credentials as a good conservative.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.410
James: Right.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:55.410
But we're doing that already.

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:59.880
That is to say that offloading to mandates
on state and local governments and on the

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:04.530
private sector, I mean, Milton's point about
43% if you add regulation in there, Milton,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.150
you get more like 50%.

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.130
Herbert: It's more, over 50% [crosstalk 00:11:15]

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.450
Henry: [crosstalk 00:11:15] with 34%, according
to the OECD.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.450
[crosstalk 00:11:19].

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.340
Ben: Let Milton come in from San Francisco
for a minute.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:15.340
Milton.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:18.760
Milton: I think this is much of this is beside
the point.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:22.040
In the first place so far as the public at
large voting on it.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:26.791
I'm making my case in the public the Balanced
Budget Amendment will only become part of

00:09:26.791 --> 00:09:31.040
the Constitution if it's accepted by three-quarters
of the states.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.890
That's surely a chance for the public to express
their opinion on it we're not trying to slip

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:36.450
anything over.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.760
In the second place, I wanna emphasize, and
I'm not a conservative.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:46.110
I'm a liberal in the good old fashioned sense
of a 19th-century liberal, more likely a libertarian.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:53.440
And as such, what I wanna do and what I believe
the public at large wants to do is in Reagan's

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.920
phrase, "Get the government off the back of
the people."

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:02.280
And the only way you can do that is by having
some kind of a limit on government spending.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:06.430
That's the purpose of requiring a 60% majority
for a tax.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.660
James: I think Milton is exactly right.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.610
One important and if not the most important
effect of the Balanced Budget Amendment would

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:17.040
be to circumscribe the growth of government.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:21.570
Mark Crane and I did some analysis of state
regulatory institutions, some states have

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.940
balanced budget requirements in the Constitution,
some have it as a matter of law, some do not

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:26.940
have it.

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:29.190
There are all sorts of different variations.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:34.940
We used as the dependent variable growth per
capita spending in the state.

00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:41.030
What we found Milton, and Herb and Henry is
that a balanced budget requirement restrains

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:46.440
the growth of spending in the States, and
this happens without regard to whether there

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:50.310
is a supermajority requirement for a tax increase.

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:55.490
Herbert: But the public and the political
process are gonna have to make a lot of very

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.020
difficult decisions about the budget.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:05.020
Decisions about the budget, that are as important
as the total size of the budget, or the size

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:08.780
of the deficit, they have to decide what is
appropriate defense policy?

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:10.370
What is appropriate welfare policy?

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.200
What is appropriate health policy?

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.131
And these questions cannot be resolved by
a constitutional amendment, and they are no

00:11:17.131 --> 00:11:19.960
different from the question about the total
size of the budget.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:20.960
Ben: Right.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:21.960
James: But the...

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:22.960
Milton: I believe they are...

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:23.960
James: But the balanced budget amendment would
enforce...

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.700
Ben: Hold on, one second I wanna just shift
gears for a moment to help our audience out.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:31.030
Let me put this question in the pot.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:38.770
Are deficits, really bad, running deficits,
year after year after year?

00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:43.060
Herbert: Well, I don't think you can answer
that question, because it depends on what

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:47.910
you're doing with the money that you raised
through the deficit.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:52.490
And of course, it's kind of, other things
equal, I think little deficits are a little

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:58.830
bad and big deficits are big bad, but I wouldn't...there
are certainly circumstances for which it is

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.980
entirely appropriate to run a deficit.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:02.000
James: Herb is right.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.040
I mean, in a perfect world you would run surpluses
sometimes, you would run deficit sometimes,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:14.200
Herb invented the full employment surplus
or balanced budget full employment with the

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.200
automatic stabilizers.

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:16.470
And all of that is...works.

00:12:16.470 --> 00:12:22.490
The question though, is we're not in a perfect
world would it make sense to prevent the politicians

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.360
in Washington from using deficit finance?

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:30.410
Ben: Jim, we've just elected your kind of
people we elected a Jim Miller, Milton Friedman,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:31.691
kind of Congress and you don't trust them?

00:12:31.691 --> 00:12:32.691
James: I don't trust them.

00:12:32.691 --> 00:12:34.020
Milton: Well, look at what's happened.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.020
Ben: Hold it.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.940
You ran for Senate in the primary in Virginia
against Oliver North, you almost beat him.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:39.940
James: Right.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.290
Ben: Would you trust you if you were there?

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:43.120
James: Of course, I would trust me [inaudible
00:14:51]

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:45.490
Ben: But you don't trust your republican conservative
colleagues.

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:46.490
Henry: I would trust him too.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:47.630
James: I don't trust the institution.

00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:51.060
Herbert: But I think that we ought to have
a surplus now.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.230
But when I asked myself, "Should we have a
surplus now?"

00:12:53.230 --> 00:12:55.140
I say, "Yes, it would be desirable.

00:12:55.140 --> 00:13:00.800
And it would be desirable because I can think
of $300 or $400 billion worth of spending

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.160
that's in the budget, that I would be willing
to get rid of in order to have the surplus.

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.160
Ben: Like what?

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:10.430
Herbert: Well, I would get rid of Medicare
I would cut Medicaid down very sharply.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:14.610
I would start phasing out Social Security
I would get rid of foreign price supports.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.111
I would get rid of the small business administration.

00:13:16.111 --> 00:13:17.111
Henry: And you won't get elected.

00:13:17.111 --> 00:13:18.111
Herbert: Well, I...

00:13:18.111 --> 00:13:19.111
Ben: Jim you wanna run on that platform?

00:13:19.111 --> 00:13:20.111
You're a conservative...

00:13:20.111 --> 00:13:22.740
James: I wanna make a lot of changes.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:23.750
Maybe not all the changes.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.420
Milton: Well, look, let's stop that.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:29.270
If you go after each one of those individually,
you will not get elected.

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:30.270
James: Right.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:33.980
Milton: If you go after them as a bundle that's
a different thing.

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:39.740
I believe the views of the American public
tend to be misunderstood.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:42.320
If you ask the public, do you wanna cut social
security?

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:43.670
They'll say no.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:50.430
But if you ask them, "Would you rather eliminate
the deficit by cutting spending or by increasing

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:51.430
taxes?"

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.640
They overwhelmingly say they wanna cut spending.

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:58.650
Herbert: Well, we've asked them now since
1936, we've been asking them in public opinion

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.510
polls, do you wanna balance the budget?

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.810
They always say, overwhelmingly, no.

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:07.710
And they always overwhelmingly elect people
who never did balance the budget, and he gave

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.760
no sign of ever wanting to balance the budget.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:14.670
I don't think the American people really care
very much about balancing the budget.

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:15.670
Ben: Right.

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:16.670
[crosstalk 00:16:23].

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:17.670
Ben: Let me...hold it we have...

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:18.670
Milton: Could I interject one point?

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:19.670
Ben: Yeah, go ahead.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:25.210
Milton: We had...we may not have balanced
the budget to the nickel from 1945 until 1974

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:31.260
the ratio of the U.S. debt to our productive
capacity declined by more than half.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:32.880
Herbert: That was the year I left the government.

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:34.920
Ben: That was the year Herbert left the government.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:35.920
Herbert: [inaudible 00:16:43]

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:40.310
James: It was then...no, it was because we
were running deficits that were much smaller

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:41.640
than the growth of the economy.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:46.650
If the deficits had been large, it would have
grown, and what happened was it then leveled

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.640
off until 1981 these are facts, Milton.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:50.740
Milton: I know.

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:57.750
James: And then in 1981, we entered an era
where we cut taxes, raised spending, and really

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:01.110
lied to the American public about the probable
fiscal consequences.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:02.110
Ben: Right.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:03.110
Milton.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:07.400
Milton: The debt declined as a fraction of
income, not because we ran small surpluses,

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:08.640
small deficits.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:12.950
But because we inflated the debt away, we
repudiated a large fraction of the debt.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:17.630
We were paying interest rates on the debt,
which was below the rate of inflation.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:19.030
That's how the debt was reduced.

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:20.030
Henry: All right.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:23.320
Let me talk about something that maybe I know
something about, which is public opinion,

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:28.030
which you all learned economists have discussed.

00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:33.470
And politics, I've been around Washington
long enough to say, you know, what is the

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.050
hidden agenda?

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:38.690
And it's not even very hidden in this discussion.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:41.950
Milton Friedman says he doesn't care if the
budget is balanced.

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:43.540
He doesn't like government.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:45.910
He says the government is picking your pocket.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.390
So why are you arguing?

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:53.280
I mean, isn't this being somewhat deceitful
is to say, "We're talking, this is good for

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:55.030
the economy, it's good for this, it's good
for that."

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:59.010
In fact, why don't you get out there and say,
"Government is bad for us, and this is what

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.500
we're talking about not a balanced budget
amendment, this is a political agenda."

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:05.170
James: Well, look, I mean, you have too much
government.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:09.730
I think that's what the election was about
balancing the budget is a very appealing to

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:14.620
people it will have the effect, as Milton
said of restraining the avarice of the federal

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:15.620
government.

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:16.620
And I think that's good.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:18.920
Ben: I thought Herbert was saying that you
can use the Balanced Budget Amendment, or

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:19.920
Henry, to go up?

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.460
James: I don't think that's correct.

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:27.030
Herbert: But I think the answer is, to your
question, is that we have a responsibility

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:30.020
to tell the American people what their choices
are.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:34.190
And if we think that the government...I believe,
to say the government is too big by itself

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:37.610
is really meaningless without specifying what
the government is doing.

00:16:37.610 --> 00:16:41.490
But I think we should confront the American
people with this choice.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:43.100
Do you want Medicare?

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:44.430
Do you want Social Security?

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:45.430
Do you want this or that?

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:46.430
Ben: All right.

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:48.640
Milton, he says, it doesn't matter the level
of government.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:49.640
What do you say?

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.220
Milton: I'm saying, Herbert what you're saying
is not fair.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:58.180
Those of us who have been...who think government
has been doing too much have specified over

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.500
and over again, what we think needs to be
done.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:07.150
Have over and over again, urged, for example,
privatization of Social Security, so that

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:10.140
it's not fair to criticize us on the ground
that we haven't...

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.470
Herbert: I'm not criticizing you, Milton,
but I'm just criticizing all these people

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:15.110
in the congress who have never said anything
like that.

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:16.790
Milton: Well, that may well be.

00:17:16.790 --> 00:17:19.770
Henry: I think it's also important you mentioned
public opinion, Ben.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:22.589
Ben: I mentioned it because you all mentioned
it.

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:23.589
Henry: Yes.

00:17:23.589 --> 00:17:24.589
Well, okay.

00:17:24.589 --> 00:17:29.810
The American public has consistently said
they want lower taxes and a balanced budget.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:33.680
They have also said when asked about specific
activities of government that they want to

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:39.230
maintain or even expand, everything that they're
asked specifically about with two exceptions

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:40.230
welfare and foreign aid.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:41.230
Ben: Right.

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:42.520
So public opinion in this field is mush.

00:17:42.520 --> 00:17:43.520
Henry: It's inconsistent.

00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:44.520
Ben: It's mush.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:45.520
Milton: It's not.

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:46.520
Henry: It's inconsistent.

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:47.520
Ben: Milton, why not?

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:50.290
Milton: The questions that are asked, because
the questions that are asked...

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:51.310
Ben: They say two things.

00:17:51.310 --> 00:17:53.650
Milton: No, excuse me for a moment.

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:58.040
If you ask a person, do you want to eliminate
one thing which he benefits from, the rational

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.640
answer is no.

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:05.290
But if you give him a package, if you give
him a bundle, and say, "Do you want... the

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.240
only way we can cut government is by cutting
a lot of things."

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.210
We had that example in connection with the
closing of the military bases you couldn't

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.430
get anyone closed when you tr to get it alone.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:20.430
When you had a package of it, ala Dick Armey's
proposal, you got bases closed.

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:21.760
Ben: Let me ask you this.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:27.490
Henry, I mean, I just asked the conservatives,
Jim Miller, and Milton Friedman, whether they...

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:28.690
Henry: I'm not a conservative.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:33.460
Ben: I'm sorry the conservative and libertarian
faction of this panel, whether they weren't

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:36.470
really promoting a not so hidden agenda.

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:41.260
They weren't just talking about the budget
amendment, but they were talking about, they

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:43.220
would like to defund the welfare state.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:46.130
And this is one way to do it.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:50.700
And my sense is that the politics on the other
side, you're all talking about, well, it would

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:54.720
be hard to adjust it and hard to regulate,
and sometimes we ought to have a deficits.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:58.730
But you guys wanna keep the welfare state
and you have to have high spending to do it.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:00.890
Is that the political agenda over there?

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:02.810
Herbert: It's not the case.

00:19:02.810 --> 00:19:06.560
It's not my case, of course, the welfare state
is a very vague term.

00:19:06.560 --> 00:19:10.550
And I would like to confine welfare to a very
small fraction of the American population

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:15.230
that is really poor, and get rid of all the
welfare that goes to the American middle class,

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:17.680
or about which everybody is now bleeding.

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:23.010
So I'm not for the welfare state in the general
sense.

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:28.680
But my agenda, I guess, really, is to have
a rational national discussion about what

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:36.630
our alternatives are in the open and offer
the American...and I think that we are capable

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:37.890
of learning to do better.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:41.940
And I would give the example of last year's
debate about health care reform.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:46.760
I think that was a case in which the options
were fairly clearly laid out, and in which

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:52.300
we did not make the same mistakes that we
made 20 or 30 years earlier, by making enormous

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:53.410
commitments to the future.

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:58.510
I think we are showing the great gain and
the prudence and responsibility of our fiscal

00:19:58.510 --> 00:19:59.510
policy.

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:02.220
And I think...so I'm hopeful that we will
continue to do that.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:06.420
James: But Herb and Henry, I think that a
balanced budget amendment would force people

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:08.110
to make those kinds of choices.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:12.740
Our politicians, as representatives of the
people, would have to weigh and assess the

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:18.280
alternatives rather than offloading the burden
on future generations because of fiscal illusion.

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:22.800
Henry: Yeah, actually, a balanced budget amendment
doesn't force any member of Congress to vote

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:27.090
against any public expenditure or in favor
of any tax.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.760
James: But it forces them to discuss it and
resolve it in some way.

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:32.570
Henry: No, it doesn't even do that.

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:36.820
They can, if they wish, ignore the amendment.

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:41.960
And if they do so you will have a situation
where somebody will take it to court, and

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.020
the Supreme Court will be setting budget policy
for the United States.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.960
James: Not under current circumstances...

00:20:46.960 --> 00:20:47.960
Henry: That's the only way.

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.480
James: Anyone that spends beyond the limit
is in violation of federal law.

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:53.480
Henry: Oh, absolutely, who judges?

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:56.360
James: It's a violation of the anti-deficiency
law and they go to jail.

00:20:56.360 --> 00:20:57.360
Henry: Oh, no.

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:58.360
Herbert: Who is that?

00:20:58.360 --> 00:21:02.720
James: The members of the Congress who are
an agency head that authorizes spending beyond

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:03.720
the limit.

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:05.900
Ben: I can see on the monitor that Milton
wants to come in from San Francisco.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:07.020
Milton: Yes, you're right.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:11.890
I do not believe it will go to the courts
I believe a properly drawn up balanced budget

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:15.590
amendment will put the power in the hands
of the Congress and the President between

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:16.590
them.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.980
And that you're not going to have all of this
nonsense that's being talked about.

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:20.980
[crosstalk 00:23:27]

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:26.230
Milton: What I don't understand, I wanna ask
those who are opposed to the Balanced Budget

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:31.600
Amendment, whether they think it's inappropriate
for the public at large, to set a limit on

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:36.490
the fraction of their income that government
is entitled to spend?

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:37.490
Henry: Absolutely not.

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:40.840
I think it is entirely proper, we have an
electoral process that is perfectly suited

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:41.840
to do that now.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:46.910
Milton: It is not, it is suited to do it on
a piecemeal basis, but not on an overall basis.

00:21:46.910 --> 00:21:52.740
Herbert: Whether it's appropriate for the
people of 1995, to decide for the people of

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:55.880
2095 what proportion of their income should
go to the government.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:57.620
And I don't think that's appropriate.

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:01.612
We're talking about forever now if we talk
about a constitutional amendment, and that

00:22:01.612 --> 00:22:02.700
I think is what this...

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:07.690
Milton: Would you think it would be wrong
for the people in 1995, to specify that the

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:12.020
federal...governments at all levels should
never spend 100% of the national income?

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:13.020
Ben: Henry.

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:16.720
Henry: I would just point out that Milton
is once again discussing something that is

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.220
not before the American public.

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:23.840
What is before the American public is an amendment
he himself has said is not well designed,

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:31.050
and that he himself would oppose, he's discussing
a different agenda not the one that is before

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:34.550
the American public today, which is the amendment
that the House of Representatives approved.

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:35.550
Ben: Milton.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:36.550
All right, go ahead, Milton.

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:39.101
Milton: That isn't so because the Senate still
has to come in.

00:22:39.101 --> 00:22:43.880
And in my opinion, I'm opposed to the present
one, because I think if you turn this down,

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:50.040
there will be enough of an outcry about turning
it down because of the tax limitation proposal,

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:55.430
that within two or three or four months, you'd
have a proper balanced budget amendment passed.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:23:01.680
I do not think the public at large is opposed
to a 60% majority rule for taxes.

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.820
James: I don't think they are either.

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:07.130
But I, on the other hand, I don't think that
you're going to get the two-thirds vote in

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:12.620
both houses that you have to have to put the
amendment before the states with the tax limitation

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:13.620
in it.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:17.800
I wish that were not the case, I would like
to even strengthen the amendment further by

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:22.970
putting a restraint on the proportion of gross
national product that government may spend,

00:23:22.970 --> 00:23:26.300
but that wish is not going to come to fruition,
I'm afraid.

00:23:26.300 --> 00:23:28.940
Henry: Just to go back to the point that Herb
made.

00:23:28.940 --> 00:23:33.850
My colleague Charles Schulz has pointed out
that the founding fathers when dealing with

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:39.130
matters of human rights, stated them with
great specificity, put them in the constitution

00:23:39.130 --> 00:23:40.820
and defined them exactly.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:46.420
In discussing procedures that would be used
inside the government they laid down hard

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.190
and fast rules.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:52.690
But when it came to setting economic policies
such as the share of gross domestic product

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:57.060
that should go to government, they said nothing.

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:03.030
They understood that these are the kinds of
issues that need to be resolved in each generation,

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:06.070
through the political process by elected officials.

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:10.880
I don't think we would want Thomas Jefferson
setting an economic policy for our age, we

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.370
would not want Alexander Hamilton setting
it.

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:14.370
Ben: Right.

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:18.030
James: But could I just say, first of all,
never in their wildest dreams would they think

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:19.880
of a government as large as we have today.

00:24:19.880 --> 00:24:24.800
And secondly, Mr. Jefferson, before he died,
wrote a friend and said he thought perhaps

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:28.520
the biggest mistake in the constitution was
allowing deficit finance.

00:24:28.520 --> 00:24:36.340
Ben: Okay, thank you, Milton Friedman, Herbert
Stein, Jim Miller, and Henry Aaron.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:37.470
And thank you.

